Our guest today is Matthias Gradle Norweg, the CEO of SciBo, the game studio behind Subway Surfers, the most downloaded mobile game of all time with over 4.5 billion lifetime downloads.

8
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:18,240
On the podcast, we talk with Matthias about building the Subwayubway Surfers marketing machine on salaries, not ad spend, leaving money on the table to protect player experience, and why more apps should try rewarded ads, season passes, and other tactics from gaming.

9
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:21,840
Hey Matthias, thank you so much for joining us on the podcast today.

10
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:24,720
Hey guys, I'm happy to join.

11
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:26,720
And Jacob, nice to have you too.

12
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:27,760
I'm here.

13
00:01:27,920 --> 00:01:29,280
Let's talk Subway Surfers.

14
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:29,680
Games.

15
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:31,600
Two CEOs on the podcast.

16
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:34,720
These are always fun for me, getting to be a fly on the wall.

17
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:42,400
If it happens like you draw a sideline a sideline, David, and just ask you a bunch of tactical questions for my own education, but I'll try not to today.

18
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:43,040
Awesome.

19
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:46,960
So I want to kick things off with the longevity of Subway Servers.

20
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:57,600
I think everyone in our audience will know what Subway Servers is, so we don't need to talk about that specifically, but it is very unique in how long it's been popular.

21
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:02,720
I mean, I think you just celebrated 4.5 billion downloads, which is just insane.

22
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:07,520
And so I'd love to hear from you, what you think has contributed to that.

23
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:19,200
I mean, this is what all all subscription apps would love to have, an all trails kind of thing where decades, Duolingo, you want to be this, like long lived, keep subscribers for a long time, keep people active.

24
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:23,200
So I think there's a lot to learn from the longevity of Subway servers.

25
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:26,960
Yeah, I think we came at the right time.

26
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:34,960
There was definitely both a timing aspect where Bode and Sylvester saw the need for next generation runner.

27
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:40,160
So Timberrun came out and designed the genre, but Sylvester and Bode perfected it.

28
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:45,680
So we can see that not only are 4.5 billion downloads, and of course half of those are reinstalls approximately.

29
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:58,400
We could see that their formula of the three lane mechanics, the swipes, the curvy kind of lift leaning horizon, all those things have become the formula for all other runners.

30
00:02:58,640 --> 00:03:02,800
So I think combined more than 15 billion downloads on that formula.

31
00:03:03,760 --> 00:03:21,920
And I think, you know, in recent years you can hardly get on TikTok without seeing Subway Surfers be part of these viral TikToks, um, how has that played into and what other strategies have you worked on over the years to kind of keep it fresh and relevant?

32
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:36,480
I think early on the guys came up with the World Tour, which was a way of every three weeks going to a new part of the world and inspiring people either to see their own hometown or a nearby town or a part of the world they hadn't traveled to.

33
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:38,480
So I think that helped us keep fresh.

34
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:52,800
And then I think the intuitive gameplay means that new players coming in because you have to imagine that in 2012 this was a game from adults to adults, but then everyone that have started playing since also plays Subway Server.

35
00:03:52,960 --> 00:03:57,520
So now we know that also a lot of kids are playing the game, but it was never designed for them.

36
00:03:57,680 --> 00:04:02,640
It was, it's just a product of a really good and intuitive game that can also be played by everyone.

37
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:26,080
It starts capping off at 45, 50, then people think it gets a bit too twitchy and a bit too hard to move the fingers, not judging, but I think overall we've been able to stay in an evergreen, first and foremost, I think we are in that bracket of Angry Birds, Subway Surface, Candy Crush, all from the Nordic countries, funny enough.

38
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:30,720
And then I think we, the founders very early on said it's about longevity.

39
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:37,280
We want to be a truly free to play app where you can play the core run as much as you want.

40
00:04:37,680 --> 00:04:38,720
We will never pinch you.

41
00:04:39,040 --> 00:04:41,120
We will never guard you from content.

42
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:43,040
Anything is achievable through playing.

43
00:04:43,840 --> 00:04:47,760
And then I think that, as you say, with TikTok, we've become a cultural phenomenon as well.

44
00:04:47,920 --> 00:05:11,440
So we can see that when Jimmy Fallon mentioned us on Tonight Show as an example of how you make sludge content, when Justin Bieber tweets us or the last week Tonight, John Oliver mentions us, that those are things that other companies would pay for and we're having the luxury of being mentioned because they think that this is how you do TikTok or this is how you work on virality, right?

45
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,480
How have you all leaned into that aspect of things?

46
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:24,240
I believe I read that you've made it very clear that folks can use your IP in video and you're not going to come after them and think that they're not going to do come after them and things like that, but are there any other ways you've leaned into that virality?

47
00:05:25,440 --> 00:05:30,160
So we've always had a strategy of producing content that could be used for UGC.

48
00:05:30,560 --> 00:05:34,720
So every user generated asset is also amplified if we think it's cool.

49
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:40,160
So not only do we allow them to do it, we also share it afterwards if they do something cool.

50
00:05:41,120 --> 00:05:42,800
The team is also very autonomous.

51
00:05:43,120 --> 00:05:51,840
So we've had a very proactive team for many years that have managed to make several viral waves where we could see that the audience really is picking up.

52
00:05:52,160 --> 00:06:00,000
But I think in my twelve years running this studio, I came the year after launch, I have said no to the marketing team twice.

53
00:06:01,280 --> 00:06:03,920
And they produce what, five, five, ten assets a day.

54
00:06:04,320 --> 00:06:08,800
So there have been very few instances where I said, maybe this is too much.

55
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:11,200
There's often very little downside to it, right?

56
00:06:11,360 --> 00:06:14,160
Like you just, if something's bad, it often just doesn't go anywhere.

57
00:06:14,320 --> 00:06:15,280
Nobody sees it anyway, right?

58
00:06:15,920 --> 00:06:18,560
Yeah, that's also why I like what they're doing.

59
00:06:19,120 --> 00:06:28,240
A cool example was we had Rami, the Hall of Famer, who also happens to be the keyboard player of Foo Fighters, produced the soundtrack for one of our cities.

60
00:06:28,960 --> 00:06:34,560
And I had agreed with him that, look, we used this picture and we know how cool you are as a musician.

61
00:06:34,720 --> 00:06:38,000
We're super honored that you wanted to jam with our in-house audio designer.

62
00:06:38,560 --> 00:06:45,440
But then the team said, Oh, we also want to make this video and send a video that I didn't know how would land with Rami.

63
00:06:46,080 --> 00:06:58,800
So sending a text to someone saying, Look, I know my team is super creative, but I don't want to step on anyone's toes and I admire all the creative work you've done and I don't want to come out as overly sassy by sending a something that just pisses anyone off.

64
00:06:59,200 --> 00:07:08,240
Thankfully he loved it, but sending a video where you're not really sure was meeting the two worlds of our professional partners and the in-house creativity.

65
00:07:08,480 --> 00:07:11,360
That was one of the more fun texts I've had to send them in the last month.

66
00:07:11,520 --> 00:07:17,440
You said in-house, I think, answers my question, but is all of your ad creative and sort of marketing creative done in-house?

67
00:07:17,840 --> 00:07:17,920
Yes.

68
00:07:18,800 --> 00:07:24,080
It's basically a content flywheel, like a furnace just spitting out things.

69
00:07:24,320 --> 00:07:25,040
They brainstorm.

70
00:07:25,600 --> 00:07:34,160
They remember to keep a balance of in-game relevanvant material, which means that we don't just want to create virality and then people don't know where it comes from.

71
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:44,320
Some of the viral videos we did in the past basically was just the team having fun, which generated a hundred million views on a video, but people didn't know that this had anything to do with Subway Surface.

72
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:52,080
It still helps us with the algorithms because of course they can see which account it comes from and they know that that account is tied to this thing.

73
00:07:52,560 --> 00:08:04,880
So when I talk to Apple and Google, they also acknowledge that part of the algorithm is of course also seeing what works on other platform on TikTok, we're also featured more in our app stores.

74
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:12,640
But the gameplay relevant material is still very important so people see the game and not just associate us with being funny, viral.

75
00:08:13,360 --> 00:08:16,240
The other content is basically just riffing off of everything that's coming.

76
00:08:16,320 --> 00:08:23,040
So whenever a TV series goes global, we also did the honeycake from Squid Games as a Subway Service logo.

77
00:08:23,600 --> 00:08:32,640
We do The Last of Us, then we take with our characters and we constantly try and when I say we, I mean the team to just write.

78
00:08:32,800 --> 00:08:35,520
Right the waves of virality because we are one of them.

79
00:08:35,760 --> 00:08:37,760
We also have a swing at Duolingo.

80
00:08:37,840 --> 00:08:39,280
Every now and then they have a swing at Duolingo.

81
00:08:39,360 --> 00:08:42,480
I was going to mention it's very similar to Duolingo's kind of strategy, right?

82
00:08:42,800 --> 00:08:43,600
Of course it's in house.

83
00:08:43,840 --> 00:08:50,080
I kind of think I knew the answer to that because you just can't outsource creative agencies can't take risks with the brand.

84
00:08:50,320 --> 00:08:53,440
Like, you know, there's just there's no incentive for them to do it, right?

85
00:08:53,840 --> 00:08:58,240
And it's just very difficult if they don't have buy in from whoever this key brand stakeholder is.

86
00:08:58,480 --> 00:08:59,840
We had a new person joining the team.

87
00:09:00,080 --> 00:09:05,120
We just bought the mascot costume for Guard that chases you in the g game.

88
00:09:05,600 --> 00:09:07,040
And she did a dance with that costume.

89
00:09:07,200 --> 00:09:12,400
And I don't know if it was her very first video, but basically one of the first videos she produced had 75 million views.

90
00:09:12,640 --> 00:09:12,880
She has.

91
00:09:13,360 --> 00:09:18,560
That's a really tough, that's a really tough start to beat because, of course, everyone was like, Oh, make one more.

92
00:09:19,040 --> 00:09:19,120
Yeah.

93
00:09:20,640 --> 00:09:23,120
Then fair to say she could say, make another game.

94
00:09:23,200 --> 00:09:24,160
It's super hard to make success.

95
00:09:24,480 --> 00:09:27,840
Yeah, much like games, content for games is a hits driven business, right?

96
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:31,120
Like, it's hard to get lightning in a bottle twice, you know?

97
00:09:31,600 --> 00:09:31,920
Exactly.

98
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:35,840
How does that look like on the paid side and how has that evolved over the years?

99
00:09:36,240 --> 00:09:39,680
Like, are you putting money behind these GGC things?

100
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:40,960
Are you paying for brand?

101
00:09:41,680 --> 00:09:44,160
Are you doing playable ads and things like that?

102
00:09:44,400 --> 00:09:48,720
Like, what's the paid strategy behind the kind of brand and content flywheel?

103
00:09:49,360 --> 00:09:51,280
The short answer is that we spend nothing.

104
00:09:51,600 --> 00:09:52,480
We spend salaries.

105
00:09:53,200 --> 00:10:01,920
So it's all organic, but it's also fair to say that we use very small budgets to boost things when we see that they go viral.

106
00:10:02,400 --> 00:10:06,160
So we do amplify it, but we're talking tens of thousands of dollars.

107
00:10:06,400 --> 00:10:10,800
We're doing it very strategically.

108
00:10:11,040 --> 00:10:14,720
When we see that some content picks up, then we might put a few hundred dollars behind it.

109
00:10:15,040 --> 00:10:18,080
Just to create other opportunities for it to go viral, right?

110
00:10:18,160 --> 00:10:18,880
Like getting it to go viral.

111
00:10:18,960 --> 00:10:19,280
Exactly.

112
00:10:19,600 --> 00:10:21,120
So it transcends different platforms.

113
00:10:21,280 --> 00:10:26,320
Because of course, if you see it on one platform, then we see that this content could perhaps ignite on other places.

114
00:10:26,480 --> 00:10:31,280
So bring it over to Instagram and then spend on some ads to get it to go viral on that platform as well.

115
00:10:31,520 --> 00:10:32,080
That makes sense.

116
00:10:32,640 --> 00:10:35,360
So, but in general, the Flywheel runs by itself.

117
00:10:35,600 --> 00:10:37,760
It's simply just them producing things.

118
00:10:38,160 --> 00:10:40,640
And also, Justin Bieber makes a video.

119
00:10:42,800 --> 00:10:47,200
They make a post where the guard says that Justin Bieber got me in a choke code for his new album.

120
00:10:47,920 --> 00:10:48,960
And then he posts that.

121
00:10:49,280 --> 00:10:54,000
And the first thing some media does is Justin Bieber hints new collab coming.

122
00:10:54,480 --> 00:10:59,600
And of course we just sit tight and don't comment because it's not our business to correct it.

123
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:00,480
Who knows?

124
00:11:00,960 --> 00:11:02,080
We would love to work with Justin.

125
00:11:02,320 --> 00:11:04,320
You should reach out if you listen to the podcast.

126
00:11:04,960 --> 00:11:05,680
It could be in the works.

127
00:11:05,840 --> 00:11:07,040
It's just a very early stages.

128
00:11:07,600 --> 00:11:07,760
You know?

129
00:11:08,720 --> 00:11:09,680
I mean, we're up.

130
00:11:09,840 --> 00:11:10,480
We're up to play.

131
00:11:11,120 --> 00:11:12,640
But it's just to say that those things also.

132
00:11:13,280 --> 00:11:28,800
live in a world that is somehow out of our control, where just the fact that we are viral gives us the ability to serve more content, but we also really have to protect the brand so we don't end up just becoming fart sounds and whatever is trending that month.

133
00:11:29,120 --> 00:11:33,520
So we didn't go all in on skipi the toilet, even though quite a few brands did, right?

134
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:38,560
Is there one person there that is the arbitr of when fart sounds is too much?

135
00:11:38,800 --> 00:11:40,240
Is it sort of like in the culture?

136
00:11:40,400 --> 00:11:43,920
Like who's guarding against that, like jumping the shark?

137
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:23,360
you are allowed to take risks, but then you don't, but you also need some safeguards, but you don't want the extreme of that, which is everything has to be by consensus, right?

138
00:12:23,600 --> 00:12:24,320
Because then you just don't.

139
00:12:24,720 --> 00:12:31,360
And that's also why I think even if you asked them, Celia and Adam, I think they would say, we often we don't often say no either.

140
00:12:31,680 --> 00:12:36,000
So it's not a vibe of approvals, it's much more, let's brainstorm.

141
00:12:36,160 --> 00:12:44,080
And then we see, for example, Hypen from a K-pop band post a video where he does the facial expressions to the sounds in the game.

142
00:12:44,240 --> 00:12:46,160
We've seen several content creators do that.

143
00:12:46,960 --> 00:12:49,440
And again, people think, well, how much did you do pay?

144
00:12:49,680 --> 00:12:53,120
And it's just that they think that this is a cool content piece to make.

145
00:12:55,040 --> 00:12:56,960
These brands really become self-sustaining at some point.

146
00:12:57,280 --> 00:13:04,960
When you think about Nintendo or Mario or something like that, they don't have to pay someone to do viral things with the Mario IP, right?

147
00:13:05,520 --> 00:13:07,920
It really does become and it's sort of self-sustaining.

148
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:11,920
And I guess like something you mentioned earlier, you're not pinching people so hard.

149
00:13:12,240 --> 00:13:17,360
You almost have to have this very efficient content creation engine.

150
00:13:17,920 --> 00:13:20,000
Like if you want not to have to pinch people, right?

151
00:13:20,160 --> 00:13:23,840
To make the game playable and a great free to play experience.

152
00:13:24,480 --> 00:13:28,880
It's also because I think the founders originally didn't want to monetize at all.

153
00:13:29,360 --> 00:13:35,280
They just wanted a game that kept staying free and playable, and therefore that would generate money.

154
00:13:36,480 --> 00:13:43,200
Of course, we have further ambitions now, and the industry has also matured, so that would not be enough in the current climate.

155
00:13:43,840 --> 00:13:54,880
But we are definitely winning or gaining from just being an Evergreen and seeing that we in the top down five, we have been since launch.

156
00:13:55,600 --> 00:14:03,200
Every year there are a couple of not every year, we have also been number one, but in most years there are a couple of positions that are taken by companies paying.

157
00:14:03,760 --> 00:14:06,320
So that's currently Roblox and Garina Freefire.

158
00:14:06,720 --> 00:14:10,320
They are above us and they're paying for their traffic, they're making a lot of money, all good.

159
00:14:10,960 --> 00:14:14,000
Then you see the occasional, the yearly trend.

160
00:14:14,240 --> 00:14:24,720
So you had Among Us, you had Piano Tiles 2, then you had another game that people remember very well from that year, but they're out the next year of top ten.

161
00:14:25,600 --> 00:14:27,920
And we're still third or fourth.

162
00:14:28,240 --> 00:14:34,880
And that means that every year we're also gaining because either the spenders will stop spending or the one year peaks will go out.

163
00:14:35,200 --> 00:14:37,200
But we are the evergreen that keeps giving.

164
00:14:37,680 --> 00:14:42,800
It's much better to be in position three not spending than to be in position one or two and having to spend a lot to be there, right?

165
00:14:43,120 --> 00:14:44,720
Like in terms of like a business, right?

166
00:14:44,880 --> 00:14:46,000
And what your margins are and everything.

167
00:14:46,160 --> 00:14:47,120
It's a much more enviable place.

168
00:14:47,680 --> 00:14:52,960
Plus then we see that, for example, in the Mario movie, not a lot of people realize that they actually also made a reference to us.

169
00:14:53,920 --> 00:14:56,560
So in the early scene where they flood the sewers.

170
00:14:57,440 --> 00:15:01,680
The newscaster starts explaining that this also affects the Subway surfing.

171
00:15:02,320 --> 00:15:07,280
And again, that's just a little love from some rider that had fun saying that this is a game.

172
00:15:08,400 --> 00:15:11,760
Let's give love to the industry and we like to do the same the other way around.

173
00:15:12,080 --> 00:15:24,400
I love that you said, you know, we don't spend much except for on salaries because a lot of people, I think, do confuse the kind of UGC and organic, you know, go viral kind of stuff.

174
00:15:24,960 --> 00:15:26,000
You're still spending on marketing.

175
00:15:26,400 --> 00:15:32,960
You're just not spending on paid the way we typically think of it, but I would imagine you have a pretty big team.

176
00:15:33,200 --> 00:15:36,960
So you're spending quite a bit on salaries and have a big team running this.

177
00:15:37,280 --> 00:15:41,600
How do you think about balancing that and how big you grow the marketing team and that sort of thing?

178
00:15:42,480 --> 00:15:44,320
So marketing for us is several things.

179
00:15:44,640 --> 00:15:50,000
There's both platform relations and growth and content and player support and so forth.

180
00:15:50,640 --> 00:15:53,600
The core part producing content is less than ten people.

181
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:54,080
Wow.

182
00:15:54,400 --> 00:15:58,320
And all the people who are super nimble and very efficient at producing.

183
00:15:58,800 --> 00:16:07,200
And therefore, I still think that we have fair salaries, but it's just to say that it's not because we're then spending a full paid marketing budget on ten individuals.

184
00:16:07,760 --> 00:16:14,320
They are themselves growing with both the creation of the ordinary process as well as the virality training.

185
00:16:14,720 --> 00:16:28,320
We had a really great talent with us for several years, and when he left, I remember thinking that how much is in him as a creator versus the strength of the brand or the virality?

186
00:16:29,120 --> 00:16:31,440
And the team definitely had to sort of readjust.

187
00:16:31,760 --> 00:16:31,840
Okay.

188
00:16:32,880 --> 00:16:34,640
These were some of the ideas that he would bring.

189
00:16:35,120 --> 00:16:37,200
What new ideas are we bringing?

190
00:16:37,760 --> 00:16:47,280
We managed the transition and of course that's a nice feeling for me as CEO that we can build on it regardless, but it's definitely also talent driven.

191
00:16:47,600 --> 00:16:53,200
So both the people that have helped us along the way and those that are helping us now are worth their own weight in gold.

192
00:16:53,520 --> 00:16:56,240
That's an incredible achievement with ten folks on the.

193
00:16:56,800 --> 00:16:58,400
I don't think you can have much bigger than that.

194
00:16:58,880 --> 00:17:02,560
Like on Mad Men, Don Draper didn't have fifty people in the room helping him co-write.

195
00:17:02,640 --> 00:17:03,120
You know what I mean?

196
00:17:03,280 --> 00:17:07,760
Like for creative processes like that, it really goes sour the bigger the group.

197
00:17:08,480 --> 00:17:14,000
Well, I wanted to transition and dig into the monetization side of things and apps.

198
00:17:14,960 --> 00:17:18,240
I feel like still have so much to learn from gaming.

199
00:17:18,480 --> 00:17:25,360
You know, we do have examples like Tinder with the consumables and multiple subscription tiers and things like that.

200
00:17:25,920 --> 00:17:33,200
But I still feel like apps are behind on the monetization curve from what mobile gaming has done this past decade.

201
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:54,160
I'd love to dig into how you think about monetization and then especially I mean, you know, as you layer on different levels of monetization, I know you have ads and then, you know, in-game currency and multiple in-game currencies and other IAP offers and there's a lot of complexity that have been layered in there.

202
00:17:54,480 --> 00:18:04,000
So I'd love to start just at a high level, like when you started monetizing, like what was the thought behind it and how did that evolve over time and then we can dig into the specifics of the economics.

203
00:18:05,360 --> 00:18:20,000
Very early on, as I say, the founders of Cybo, Southwestern Bodhi beliehi believed in having a very simple, just pure game experience, and that if people kept playing that, they would watch some ads, and by watching ads, it would pay the bills.

204
00:18:20,720 --> 00:18:35,920
The discussions we had with the publisher for the first eight years, Kilu Games in Aarhus, were much more on the side of them saying, well, we could also put in these things, but they also generally liked the approach of a truly free game that could monetize through ads.

205
00:18:36,560 --> 00:18:40,400
Because we've been live since 2012, it also means that we can't.

206
00:18:40,400 --> 00:18:49,760
It cannot be as sophisticated as you see some of the other games because that would alienate a lot of the elder players that basically just like the way the game works today.

207
00:18:50,480 --> 00:18:59,840
So we just this morning launched the biggest mobile co-lab, I think, ever with Brawl Stars, where Brawl Stars visit Subway Surfers and Subway Surfers visit Brawl Stars.

208
00:19:00,560 --> 00:19:04,400
And that's one way where we can say, look, you still have the core run to yourself.

209
00:19:05,440 --> 00:19:11,120
It follows the environment of a Brawl Stars universe, but in a month's time we will visit a new city.

210
00:19:12,080 --> 00:19:38,800
But the game mode that we do with Brawl Stars is a way of also seeing how are the players actually engaging with us, both playing with other IPs, as well as trying out game modes that are so clearly an event for a duration of time to see if that scares away people, do they stop playing the core run, how are they engaging with the PvP mechanic, and does that allow us to monetize those players in a different way than they are in the game.

211
00:19:39,200 --> 00:19:44,400
Because Subway Service obviously is constructed in a way where if you play for an hour.

212
00:19:45,200 --> 00:19:51,520
without dying and you shut down the app when you die, then you haven't paid anything for your entertainment.

213
00:19:52,160 --> 00:20:12,400
And I think in the future, at some point, we have to figure out what we do with that because that's unfair to the players that die a lot that would then see more ads because they would probably feel that the experience is worth while those that play five hours perfectly would actually not contribute to paying the salaries of those who make the product.

214
00:20:12,720 --> 00:20:16,400
So it's a constant sort of balancing act of saying, how do we?

215
00:20:17,120 --> 00:20:31,840
entertain as many players as possible with something as available as possible, but also allow those who want to spend time or want to spend money to progress or get more content to do so without the expense of ruining the fun for the majority?

216
00:20:32,960 --> 00:20:38,960
I read in an interview you did that somewhere between 80 and 85 percent of revenue still to this day comes from advertising.

217
00:20:39,200 --> 00:20:39,680
Is that correct?

218
00:20:39,840 --> 00:20:39,920
Yes.

219
00:20:40,400 --> 00:20:40,480
Yes.

220
00:20:40,960 --> 00:20:41,040
Wow.

221
00:20:41,600 --> 00:20:44,320
So it's a mix of interstitials and rewarded videos.

222
00:20:44,800 --> 00:20:46,400
What do you think about those rewarded videos?

223
00:20:46,560 --> 00:21:00,080
This is something I've wanted to seen some subscription apps experimenting with this, but it's such an interesting mechanic to allow someone to pay with their attention versus paying with money.

224
00:21:00,880 --> 00:21:03,520
And it's something that is very common in games.

225
00:21:03,920 --> 00:21:09,840
Again, I can't think off the top of my head any subscription apps that are doing this, but how do you think about that mechanic?

226
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:14,800
And how do you think about that mechanic not breaking the game or alienating users?

227
00:21:14,960 --> 00:21:23,920
As you said, you kind of already alluded to it that if you could play for an hour and never see an ad, that maybe that's different too much or ten minutes or five minutes is too little.

228
00:21:24,240 --> 00:21:31,120
How do you think about not completely breaking the experience with those rewarded ads and what maybe subscription apps can learn from that?

229
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:49,120
I think the conversations we have around monetization regarding the fairness of when is it fair to require payment and whether payment is then through a rewarded video or a subscription or a premium feature or an in-app purchase.

230
00:21:50,320 --> 00:22:02,640
There is no right answer, especially when you have a player base like like us of between 100 and 150 million people every month, we know that we're not talking to a player persona, we're talking to all types of players at all times in all markets.

231
00:22:03,440 --> 00:22:14,720
And that means that for me what the ads allow us to do is to also be available and support markets that would not traditionally have subscription apps or user in their purchases.

232
00:22:15,360 --> 00:22:24,320
So tier two, tier three, where most other big brands would not advertise and therefore not have a lot of downloads, we are still the most downloadable games in those territories.

233
00:22:25,280 --> 00:22:50,160
But specifically for the US and for Tier 1, for Europe and for the kind of higher earning countries, of course we would like to have a share of the world and to know that if you're paying in all your other games and you think, well, it's nice over here that it's free, we would love to share with you the characters or the unlocks that would sort of convert you and take part of the salary payments here and the kind of business of Cybo.

234
00:22:50,800 --> 00:22:54,000
So from that point of view, it is definitely an ongoing conversation.

235
00:22:54,720 --> 00:23:16,320
Where do we have the real estate on screen to show a banner or launch a reward video and where do we actually allow if you've spent money in the game to remove some of the interstitials and whether that should be permanently or whether that should only be for a period of time to say, well, if you paid then you won't have any distractions that you haven't chosen yourself.

236
00:23:17,360 --> 00:23:20,960
And those types of balancing acts are ever ongoing.

237
00:23:21,760 --> 00:23:30,240
You used the word fair, which is I think what a lot of developers do implicitly when they're think trying to be like, okay, what is a fair price?

238
00:23:30,480 --> 00:23:38,640
In this case, you're not talking about dollars for software, you're talking about like attention in exchange for software, as well as like blending that into dollars, right?

239
00:23:39,200 --> 00:23:41,440
Which is really challenging, right?

240
00:23:41,840 --> 00:23:44,960
I think this is one of the reasons that app developers have avoided this.

241
00:23:45,120 --> 00:23:48,640
I think one is the market has not been kind of primed for it versus gaming.

242
00:23:48,880 --> 00:23:51,760
It's been like this since as long as mobile gaming is a thing.

243
00:23:52,320 --> 00:23:53,760
It's just complicated, right?

244
00:23:54,080 --> 00:23:55,200
It's complicated to blend.

245
00:23:55,680 --> 00:24:00,160
You have an ad network and there's a third party driving that, and then you're figuring out how it fits into your experience.

246
00:24:00,480 --> 00:24:02,720
And then, and then of course you have your own monetization.

247
00:24:03,040 --> 00:24:08,080
Something you mentioned that kind of highlights how challenging this is, is like, you know, only showing ads to someone when they die.

248
00:24:08,720 --> 00:24:13,760
Probably your best and most engaged and maybe the most likely to pay people are those who don't die, right?

249
00:24:14,080 --> 00:24:18,000
So you've actually completely blocked off monetization from them, right?

250
00:24:18,160 --> 00:24:21,600
So, yeah, you have to figure out what, what, you know, how can and what, what can we sell them?

251
00:24:21,760 --> 00:24:27,600
And then, you know, to some degree, another thing they bring to you, which is not necessarily their attention, but it's their word of mouth, right?

252
00:24:27,760 --> 00:24:34,480
And so if you're extracting from them, are they going to be as likely to share you on TikTok and get you your next video, you know, ten users, right?

253
00:24:35,360 --> 00:24:38,960
And we still have thirteen years in, we still have a million daily new installs.

254
00:24:39,200 --> 00:24:41,120
And as I say, half of those are reinstalls.

255
00:24:41,760 --> 00:24:52,000
But that means that it's also worth for us to keep the content furnace and the virality going and not jeopardize it by trying to extract too much value.

256
00:24:52,960 --> 00:24:59,680
I think that's that's part of the discussion we have is that okay, with these features we can try these new mechanics.

257
00:25:00,560 --> 00:25:02,560
With the core run, we want to keep it clean.

258
00:25:03,360 --> 00:25:12,800
But then of course we see more and more more game companies saying, Well, now you need a short break from the game, we'll show you an ad, and that helps pay for the business that serves this game to you.

259
00:25:13,200 --> 00:25:14,880
I actually think that's fair as well.

260
00:25:15,120 --> 00:25:17,040
I have a nine year old son myself.

261
00:25:17,520 --> 00:25:21,760
When he watches an ad, I'm also explaining to him that this is the way that we pay for that game.

262
00:25:22,400 --> 00:25:23,360
Entertainment is business.

263
00:25:24,160 --> 00:25:26,240
No one goes to the cinema and doesn't expect to pay.

264
00:25:27,280 --> 00:25:39,040
So the fact that ads came in, and I know that that annoys some people, the premium segment that just would like a core experience from story driven, sort of A to Z., that's fine, fine by me.

265
00:25:39,520 --> 00:25:46,400
I like the fact that in mobile gaming you can actually play a lot of games without spending and you use a part of your time to watch games.

266
00:25:46,560 --> 00:25:55,520
And then the playable ads are now so fun that people actually also sometimes just get stuck in playing a quick level of some other game trying to advertise now.

267
00:25:56,400 --> 00:25:57,920
I got stuck in that loop yesterday.

268
00:25:58,480 --> 00:26:01,680
I was playing Subway Surfers and you didn't know we were going to talk today.

269
00:26:02,320 --> 00:26:04,480
And one of the playable ads was so fun.

270
00:26:05,200 --> 00:26:08,000
I spent probably five minutes playing the playable ad.

271
00:26:08,720 --> 00:26:10,560
And then came back to the game.

272
00:26:11,040 --> 00:26:13,440
But I'm glad you brought up your nine year old son.

273
00:26:13,680 --> 00:26:18,160
How do you think about protecting your brand in the ads that you serve?

274
00:26:18,480 --> 00:26:25,360
You know, I had a freemium weather app and I ended up removing ads because I was having so much trouble filtering.

275
00:26:25,600 --> 00:26:31,760
I would get comments, you know, bad reviews on the App Store, emails to support saying, you know, why are you showing ads for guns?

276
00:26:31,920 --> 00:26:33,040
And I'm like, what the heck?

277
00:26:33,200 --> 00:26:39,360
And I'm going into Google, you know, AdWords and trying to turn off like all to like make it as age appropriate as possible.

278
00:26:39,600 --> 00:26:42,960
And then I'd get another one., like, why are you doing gambling ads?

279
00:26:43,120 --> 00:26:45,200
Because it would have like the slot machine ads.

280
00:26:45,520 --> 00:26:47,440
And I go and try and figure out how to turn that off.

281
00:26:47,600 --> 00:26:54,000
So how do you approach keeping those ads kind of brand safe and kid safe, since so many kids are playing the game?

282
00:26:54,640 --> 00:27:01,840
So we're doing two things, and now it gets a little technical, but obviously when you install the game you show your age.

283
00:27:02,560 --> 00:27:08,800
And if you are younger than the legal age in your country, then you get placed in what we call the copper segment.

284
00:27:09,600 --> 00:27:11,840
Depending on the country, it's 16 or 13.

285
00:27:12,640 --> 00:27:20,560
And if you're in the copper group, then you are only served copper ads and it's only certain ad networks that have copper groups.

286
00:27:20,880 --> 00:27:24,400
So they are not showing anything that would not be copper compliance.

287
00:27:25,280 --> 00:27:39,040
But even in the adult version, if you say that you're older than that age, and I'm saying you can still not, you should not be able to see ads for games, for guns, for pornography, for anything that is R-rated.

288
00:27:39,760 --> 00:27:43,040
And that's because we don't believe that that's the way we should be doing business.

289
00:27:43,760 --> 00:27:49,040
When I say should not, it's because, of course, the ad networks sometimes make mistakes.

290
00:27:49,200 --> 00:28:09,200
And when you're a shower of a billion impressions a month, as we are, then, of course, I'm sure the viewers of your podcast could come with an example of, well, once I saw this ad, and we would actually actively go into that network and say, this is a mistake, remove this because we have heard from a player that this ad was shown.

291
00:28:09,440 --> 00:28:10,800
That's not how we want to make our money.

292
00:28:11,440 --> 00:28:18,160
So we are also very proactive in the sense that we've blacklisted a lot of cat categories that should not be shown.

293
00:28:18,640 --> 00:28:22,480
We've done all the CAPA compliance and GDPR compliance.

294
00:28:23,280 --> 00:28:28,880
And then we also make sure that if anyone says, I was shown this, that we also flag that to the ad networks.

295
00:28:29,120 --> 00:28:42,960
Yeah, I was going to say, you must have a team that's just dedicated to, well, one responding when a customer writes, but then like figuring out what ad networks you are and are not working with, and like, you know, what's your, obviously, you know, expectations for yield and stuff like that.

296
00:28:43,200 --> 00:28:46,000
But it must be a substantial amount of effort.

297
00:28:46,560 --> 00:28:52,800
It's a very efficient system and we have very few complaints., so we're actually not, we're not a lot of people doing it, but they do it really well.

298
00:28:53,440 --> 00:29:03,920
And then I think because we also have SDKs in the game that allow us to show what ads were they shown, hard nine that shows us that these were the ads that people engaged with the most, and these are the ones that they didn't.

299
00:29:04,240 --> 00:29:10,640
Are you seeing this, like, outside of the ad network's own analytics, you all tracking to, like, which ads are performing the best?

300
00:29:11,040 --> 00:29:12,880
Basically, which ad is prompted?

301
00:29:13,520 --> 00:29:13,600
Okay.

302
00:29:14,320 --> 00:29:14,400
Yeah.

303
00:29:14,640 --> 00:29:19,760
Because they can be very kind of black boxy, right, sometimes, like, how the auction resolves and all this stuff.

304
00:29:19,920 --> 00:29:20,720
Yeah, that was my problem.

305
00:29:21,040 --> 00:29:23,600
And then how do you think about, I mean, you're a team of one, David.

306
00:29:24,080 --> 00:29:24,320
I know.

307
00:29:24,560 --> 00:29:25,200
It's challenging.

308
00:29:26,320 --> 00:29:27,040
It was so challenging.

309
00:29:27,200 --> 00:29:29,120
I just, I just gave up and pulled it out.

310
00:29:30,160 --> 00:29:36,800
But the other thing I found really fascinating, you know, as a weather app developer, I download a ton of other weather apps and check them out.

311
00:29:36,960 --> 00:29:43,680
One of the fascinating things there is you will see ads for other weather apps in most weather apps.

312
00:29:44,480 --> 00:29:47,040
So how do you think about and prevent cannibalization?

313
00:29:48,080 --> 00:29:57,600
Like me yesterday, I spent a minute or two playing this other game that was prompting me to go to the App Store and download that game instead of Subway Surfers.

314
00:29:57,920 --> 00:30:06,240
And so, how do you think about that aspect of cannibalization that you may lose a player to this other game that was advertised inside Subway Surfers?

315
00:30:07,040 --> 00:30:08,640
I mean, that's the business of free to play.

316
00:30:09,200 --> 00:30:16,080
So, we know that the cross promotion or the potential cannibalization of an ad prompting you to leave is a risk.

317
00:30:16,640 --> 00:30:19,840
We treasure the work that the ad networks are doing to make money for us.

318
00:30:20,240 --> 00:30:28,800
But of course, sometimes they do it so well that we also see our retention drop a bit because of David chooses to go into another game that he finds more funny.

319
00:30:29,520 --> 00:30:53,840
Our confidence in our product and also the longevity that we're proving means that we're also confident that you will come back, that you in your next playsession sit down and don't join the game you download necessarily, but you remember why you play Subway Service and why it's one of the games that you remind yourself of when you're in the Subway, flying somewhere on a bus with no connectivity, then you're like, that's the game that you open.

320
00:30:54,480 --> 00:31:02,160
But it is definitely a thing that we have had two instances of of very direct sort of competitive titles just when Subway Surfers came out.

321
00:31:02,560 --> 00:31:07,200
Singer did a game called Running with Friends, which was a complete copy.

322
00:31:07,440 --> 00:31:20,480
It was a bull chasing you in Pamplona instead of the guard, and we were actually quite not afraid but aware and concerned that what does this mean to Subway Surfers if one of the big games comes in and just sweeps the market.

323
00:31:21,200 --> 00:31:22,080
But it wasn't as fun.

324
00:31:22,640 --> 00:31:39,440
So it went away quite quickly because people could tell that Subway Surfers and Soleste and Bodhi had cracked something about the tactility of the game and some of the intuitive nature of it that cannot just be copied page by page, but needs specific kind of metrics that we keep as our secret source.

325
00:31:40,000 --> 00:31:47,280
And then we have King came with Crash Bandicoot, which we knew that they wanted to make a runner that would monetize.

326
00:31:47,680 --> 00:31:49,360
They had put one of the best teams on it.

327
00:31:49,600 --> 00:31:50,800
The IP is known.

328
00:31:51,520 --> 00:31:52,640
The game looked amazing.

329
00:31:53,600 --> 00:31:55,600
And they did a very hard monetization.

330
00:31:55,760 --> 00:31:59,680
And we thought this is interesting because, of course, we would also like to do a runner that monetizes.

331
00:32:00,240 --> 00:32:06,320
And if they came in and took just half of our downloads but made two dollars per player, then that should have been us, right?

332
00:32:07,440 --> 00:32:09,040
It's a good experiment for you to watch, right?

333
00:32:09,760 --> 00:32:10,320
It was, yeah.

334
00:32:10,720 --> 00:32:25,760
And I mean, we actually had the talk should we tell the ad networks that please don't show ads for Crash Bandicoot, because that's probably the only game where we don't want people to then get excited and leave the game and then find something that was then better designed for monetization.

335
00:32:26,880 --> 00:32:29,120
In the end, we actually didn't do anything.

336
00:32:29,440 --> 00:32:32,000
And it turned out that, again, Software Surface is just the gold standard.

337
00:32:32,720 --> 00:32:35,200
It's good as Crash Bandicoot is his IP.

338
00:32:35,360 --> 00:32:38,320
I kind of think Subway Surfers might be a little more relevant in 2025.

339
00:32:39,280 --> 00:32:39,680
That's true.

340
00:32:39,760 --> 00:32:40,160
That's true.

341
00:32:40,240 --> 00:32:40,800
That's not me.

342
00:32:40,960 --> 00:32:46,240
But I can say that we're super happy that we are still the go-to runner.

343
00:32:46,800 --> 00:32:48,640
We consider ourselves the king of runners.

344
00:32:49,120 --> 00:32:54,880
And of course, we hope with future launches that we have other types of runners than just Subway one.

345
00:32:55,520 --> 00:33:01,440
Can I ask you, you talked about this, you just launched today this promo with Supercell and Brawl Stars.

346
00:33:02,080 --> 00:33:04,560
Yeah, I mean, that's a classic example of you can't play.

347
00:33:05,040 --> 00:33:06,240
both games at the same time, right?

348
00:33:06,640 --> 00:33:12,160
So I'm assuming we won't get into too much specific, but like how long does a deal like that take?

349
00:33:12,400 --> 00:33:16,240
And like how do you think about approaching those and working with other companies and stuff like that?

350
00:33:17,120 --> 00:33:22,640
The initial exploration for deals like that usually starts a year before, at least a year before.

351
00:33:23,440 --> 00:33:28,080
It starts with high level concepting and sort of these brands would be cool.

352
00:33:28,320 --> 00:33:29,680
We also did it with Crossiroad.

353
00:33:30,080 --> 00:33:33,520
We've just started with Eight Ball Pool, which is of course part of the Miniclip family.

354
00:33:34,400 --> 00:33:36,720
And we have other ideas for the rest of the year or next year.

355
00:33:38,160 --> 00:33:43,040
The concepting is sometimes challenging to explain because what is concepting?

356
00:33:43,840 --> 00:33:51,520
Well, that's a guy throwing ideas up on a whiteboard that eventually sparks the interest of a partner that says, We want to do that with you.

357
00:33:52,000 --> 00:33:52,800
How do we make it happen?

358
00:33:53,120 --> 00:33:55,280
Then you put engineers and legal and everyone.

359
00:33:55,600 --> 00:34:00,640
Do you just start sending them ideas or do you like to get to one of your creative people, one of their creative people in a room?

360
00:34:00,800 --> 00:34:01,600
Like, what's the process?

361
00:34:02,080 --> 00:34:06,560
I carry a lot of the outward BD, so the business development side.

362
00:34:06,960 --> 00:34:08,720
I attend conferences, I speak from stage.

363
00:34:09,360 --> 00:34:11,760
The mobile space is a relatively small industry.

364
00:34:12,480 --> 00:34:13,520
So we also know each other.

365
00:34:13,920 --> 00:34:15,600
And you're all in the same, like, five countries.

366
00:34:15,840 --> 00:34:16,480
So that's helpful.

367
00:34:16,880 --> 00:34:17,280
That helps too.

368
00:34:17,600 --> 00:34:19,040
It's helpful to fly to Finland.

369
00:34:19,360 --> 00:34:22,800
But it's also from the friendly atmosphere.

370
00:34:23,280 --> 00:34:27,600
We don't consider each other competitors because we want people to enjoy games.

371
00:34:28,160 --> 00:34:30,240
And if they're playing my game, they will also play yours.

372
00:34:30,400 --> 00:34:32,000
And they will come back to mine if mine is still free.

373
00:34:32,320 --> 00:34:36,160
Yeah, it's much less of a finite set of customers like B2B can be, right?

374
00:34:36,320 --> 00:34:38,080
Like there's only so many companies in the world.

375
00:34:38,400 --> 00:35:04,880
Yeah, even I think the weather is actually a good example because you might be using only one weather app and then if you really have a wedding or around birthday then you might check two apps but you have the habit of only opening one once a day to check which clothes you should wear where games is much more of a fluid activity during the day and if you are going on the bus or the metro, Subway Service is likely one of the games that are open because you lose connectivity sometimes.

376
00:35:05,680 --> 00:35:11,200
But if you sit at home and you bring out the console then we also know that we're not people's primary game.

377
00:35:11,520 --> 00:35:12,960
We are the game that they play in the game.

378
00:35:12,960 --> 00:35:26,240
that they play between the other sessions because they spend money and time in GTA or Call of Duty or Sims, and then they play Subway Servers before falling asleep or because they have time in the preceding sessions, right?

379
00:35:26,720 --> 00:35:38,720
So I think from that point of view we can also allow things to be a bit more freely, and therefore, back to your question, a year before when we start pitching high level ideas, it's not very concrete.

380
00:35:39,360 --> 00:35:43,440
And then as the teams start working together, of course, it becomes more and more concrete.

381
00:35:43,760 --> 00:35:50,560
How willing are we willing on each side to do something in our own game or is it more of an activation for the other title?

382
00:35:51,040 --> 00:35:53,680
And with Crossy Road it was a very sincere collaboration.

383
00:35:54,400 --> 00:36:08,560
It also has been with Eight Ball Pool and Supercell because the teams on both sides are excited to work with the other IP and we see a huge overlap in players that know both titles and are excited to see how does that look like in the game I play.

384
00:36:09,120 --> 00:36:11,280
I also know the other game and I also want to try that out.

385
00:36:11,600 --> 00:36:18,400
So we think there is a lot of also reactivation of players that would come back just for the fun of it and see what that looks like in the other game.

386
00:36:18,720 --> 00:36:20,480
Are the deals, like, fairly standard?

387
00:36:20,880 --> 00:36:26,960
Is a lot of time spent on, like, the business economics of it or is there a pretty, you know, standard understanding of what is industry norm?

388
00:36:27,360 --> 00:36:29,360
Actually, these deals have been pretty bad.

389
00:36:30,400 --> 00:36:32,320
So we're exchanging traffic.

390
00:36:33,600 --> 00:36:42,640
We're saying, look, if we send players your way and you send players our way, then what you monetize and what we monetize is different, but it boosts both of our numbers.

391
00:36:42,960 --> 00:36:46,960
So we actually haven't made any commitments to each other other other other than delivery of quality.

392
00:36:47,360 --> 00:36:48,720
Depending on what you're going to do and more.

393
00:36:49,120 --> 00:36:49,920
I guess it makes sense.

394
00:36:50,000 --> 00:36:52,000
You kind of mentioned that both brands have to work with each other.

395
00:36:52,160 --> 00:36:55,680
You almost have to be somewhat co equal, right, in sense for it to be interesting for both parties.

396
00:36:55,760 --> 00:37:05,600
I've always found that with partnerships generally is that if the parties are not of somewhat equal size, someone gets crushed or someone is uninterested or it's just very difficult.

397
00:37:06,160 --> 00:37:08,000
Not for any reason, right?

398
00:37:08,320 --> 00:37:09,600
It just kind of happens that way.

399
00:37:10,080 --> 00:37:12,000
We have a lot more downloads than Crossery Road.

400
00:37:12,880 --> 00:37:17,680
But Crossery Road did an amazing effort in their game to integrate Subway Service.

401
00:37:18,240 --> 00:37:31,840
So I know that they got a bigger bump in their downloads than we did in ours, but it didn't feel unfair and I think it actually also helped with the virality of both games in the long term that you just see games that love each other and teams that love each other.

402
00:37:32,240 --> 00:37:35,120
Like just to get their IP and your IP is positive, right?

403
00:37:35,600 --> 00:37:49,840
And with Brawl Stars specifically, I know that they make a higher LTV than we do, but that's also their day to day, so it's not as if we're coming in and tripling their LTV, but they will probably make more money per player than we do also after this game.

404
00:37:50,640 --> 00:37:56,160
We just like that we can create something cool together that is authentic and native to the brand.

405
00:37:57,280 --> 00:38:01,200
There's something I hope we see more of in the subscription app space.

406
00:38:01,440 --> 00:38:02,720
Strava just bought Runna.

407
00:38:03,600 --> 00:38:07,760
And, you know, when that initially happened, when I saw the news, I was like, Oh, that makes a lot of sense.

408
00:38:07,920 --> 00:38:11,920
They're just going to like integrate the running features and what makes Runna Runna into Strava.

409
00:38:12,240 --> 00:38:14,400
But no, it doesn't seem like that's a strategy at all.

410
00:38:14,560 --> 00:38:18,800
They bought Runna and now are pushing people back and forth between the two apps.

411
00:38:18,960 --> 00:38:21,520
And Runna is like a great brand in and of itself.

412
00:38:22,080 --> 00:38:23,440
And they're working together like that.

413
00:38:23,600 --> 00:38:37,040
In that case, it was an acquisitionquisition, but I hope to see more of this among those kinds of apps where there's with that one, maybe there was a little bit too much conflict for them to not buy and just do a partnership.

414
00:38:37,440 --> 00:38:41,440
Or it might actually be easier, depending, you know, sometimes BD can be just difficult, right?

415
00:38:41,680 --> 00:38:42,960
Maybe it's just easier to buy the company.

416
00:38:43,360 --> 00:38:44,320
It's a meeting of minds.

417
00:38:44,560 --> 00:38:51,360
When we have these meetings, also the partnerships we haven't announced, it's just exciting to see the creative teams getting it off together.

418
00:38:51,840 --> 00:38:54,160
And then seeing that what comes out excites people.

419
00:38:54,400 --> 00:38:58,640
We've got a ton of love from people saying, wow, we can't imagine you got Brawl Stars.

420
00:38:58,960 --> 00:39:05,200
And we're getting the same feedback from our Finnish counterparts at Superstars saying, people saying, we can't believe you got Subway Service.

421
00:39:06,080 --> 00:39:10,240
So it's also just a synergistic of one plus one equals three, right?

422
00:39:10,640 --> 00:39:13,760
Yeah, I mean, the music industry figured this out a while ago, right?

423
00:39:14,080 --> 00:39:15,760
Is that just collabs help, right?

424
00:39:16,000 --> 00:39:19,360
If you can get more names on a track, it just, almost only upside, right?

425
00:39:19,760 --> 00:39:22,160
To bring in other people's fans and trade the music back and forth.

426
00:39:22,320 --> 00:39:24,240
And it's a similar economy.

427
00:39:24,400 --> 00:39:25,280
It's like an attention.

428
00:39:25,840 --> 00:39:49,040
There's much more elastic demand, I feel like, in attention than in a lot of of different categories, which is maybe why we haven't seen it in apps so much, because like for a utility, you're filling a niche and like there may be only, to your point about weather apps, there may be only like room for one or two apps in someone's life, but as apps become, we're in this phase right now where apps are just proliferating at an incredible rate into more and more niches that might become less true, right?

429
00:39:49,280 --> 00:39:53,760
As the niches become more narrow, then suddenly like you can collaborate between adjacent niches.

430
00:39:53,840 --> 00:39:57,920
I think the hard part will be gaming is like a decade ahead of apps.

431
00:39:58,720 --> 00:39:59,680
It's kind of wild, right?

432
00:39:59,760 --> 00:40:16,320
It's just, yeah., like considering we kind of both started at the same time, I guess like gaming, maybe you could count the Nokia days, right, as like a head start, but it always feels like we're apps are just a little bit behind and we're still figuring things out, so maybe it's a good thing to just look and see what you're all doing and expect that we'll be there in a decade.

433
00:40:16,800 --> 00:40:22,640
Gaming has been at the forefront of monetization since I joined the industry in 2013.

434
00:40:23,760 --> 00:40:26,400
It's an amazing industry to follow.

435
00:40:27,120 --> 00:40:29,200
And of course, sometimes people also take it too far.

436
00:40:30,080 --> 00:40:36,160
Right now with apps in general, I think I see the trend of, of course, subscription is the best way to monetize.

437
00:40:36,880 --> 00:40:44,160
It also sometimes feels annoying that in order to scan this document, then you have to keep subscribing to some utility app.

438
00:40:44,480 --> 00:40:48,640
And then eventually, of course, Apple and Google allow another app that is free to do it.

439
00:40:49,200 --> 00:40:52,800
So the notes app allows you to scan, and a lot of people know that.

440
00:40:53,040 --> 00:40:59,680
So they still subscribe to some scanner app because they had one document they had to send to the state, right?

441
00:41:00,320 --> 00:41:25,760
So I think there is definitely also a longevity factor for apps, especially utility apps or apps that serve a specific thing where I would as a payer prefer to the premium model or a yearly model that is more fair than right now where you feel like everything is $100 for a year of using something that is like, yeah, but then it means I cancel.

442
00:41:26,160 --> 00:41:31,840
If it was priced more friendly to the consumer, then people would actually perhaps not cancel it.

443
00:41:32,160 --> 00:41:38,640
That's the balancing act that games because it's been so massive for so many years and we have so many g people, our players.

444
00:41:39,360 --> 00:41:44,800
It's also found its equilibrium in the different aspects of this is what players are willing to spend.

445
00:41:45,200 --> 00:41:58,640
Well, I think apps for me, in some aspects, where I end up spending too much because I have one day's need of something, and therefore, I don't become a user of other than the Strava of the world because you're a runner or because you do these things.

446
00:41:59,040 --> 00:42:08,960
That's actually where I wanted to take things next is, and again, speaking of like being so far ahead of the app industry, the idea of a season pass in apps and apps.

447
00:42:08,960 --> 00:42:23,440
And all the in-app purchases and the rewarded ads, like all this hybrid monetization, I think we will see the industry start to adopt over time where, hey, you just need one scan, okay, five bucks, like just make it happen and like, you know, you're done.

448
00:42:24,080 --> 00:42:29,600
Or slopes is we had the founder of that on the podcast and he now does a season pass.

449
00:42:29,840 --> 00:42:36,240
It's like, hey, if you just season pass, day pass, they do a bunch of like, buy as little as as as much as you want.

450
00:42:36,720 --> 00:42:43,040
But then they also have, to your point, they have the subscription for people who are avid skiers and who are going to go out six times a year or whatever.

451
00:42:43,440 --> 00:42:50,080
And so they kind of, you know, they're a good example of kind of making this work however the user wants to pay and how frequent.

452
00:42:50,480 --> 00:42:51,760
This is the advantage games have though.

453
00:42:51,920 --> 00:42:54,720
I mean, like you all you start with a store, right?

454
00:42:54,880 --> 00:42:57,600
Like you don't start with a single IAP, you have a whole store.

455
00:42:58,240 --> 00:43:09,200
And like so adding like a little thing here and slice there, it's just like there's just from the gate, games always start with like a higher level of sophistication or monetization which makes this stuff easier and apps.

456
00:43:09,360 --> 00:43:18,000
And this is something like in our mission at Runicamp, like we're exploring now is like., is this, is the reason this isn't in apps related to that inherent complexity?

457
00:43:18,320 --> 00:43:38,240
And can we bring these tools to the app developers sooner so they, I don't know what's going to work, but like, you know, if we can remove the technical barrier, at least like that would let people experience, as you were mentioning, finding that equilibrium, because like, I think apps in general to your point, like, have kind of left the, the freemium user out in a lot of cases, right?

458
00:43:38,480 --> 00:43:42,720
Either have some like very heavily ad monetized thing, which often don't work very well, right?

459
00:43:42,960 --> 00:43:44,640
There's very few because I think.

460
00:43:45,040 --> 00:43:54,160
they also just don't generate as much revenue as like a good high paid subscription app for the subscription apps, like it just doesn't generate as much, right?

461
00:43:54,400 --> 00:43:58,080
It's harder to work ads in and they haven't developed these sophisticated models.

462
00:43:58,160 --> 00:44:07,120
So like, I don't know, maybe there's maybe there's the underlying difference in like demand elasticity and amount of need for utility versus like need for entertainment.

463
00:44:07,360 --> 00:44:12,560
Maybe they'll always be different, but I I would almost equally bet that we might see these things like converge.

464
00:44:12,880 --> 00:44:12,960
Yeah.

465
00:44:13,360 --> 00:44:20,320
And my specific question around that too is like, how do you think about the season pass pass and have you explored the idea of subscriptions?

466
00:44:20,960 --> 00:44:23,360
Some games are starting to do.

467
00:44:23,760 --> 00:44:25,600
But yes, how do you think about balancing all that?

468
00:44:26,240 --> 00:44:47,440
What I really like about, and this is actually probably super self introducing the season pass, at least the way we saw it in Clash Royale, what felt really fair about it, and why I've spent a lot of money on season passes and games, is that I also know that I don't forget about it, and then I'm charged forever.

469
00:44:48,080 --> 00:44:55,360
So we all have these discoveries where we realize we've been spending $100 more than we wanted on something that we haven't used since last March.

470
00:44:56,160 --> 00:45:06,000
And that's an annoying feeling where the season pass gives you a very clear overview of these are the things you can get in this period of time if you spend this amount of money.

471
00:45:06,640 --> 00:45:10,560
And then it requires you to engage with the app or the game in this case.

472
00:45:11,120 --> 00:45:15,760
And it requires you to play to get to it, but it unlockes a separate track of rewards.

473
00:45:16,000 --> 00:45:34,480
And I think that's a really fair way of saying because when I think back to my challen childhood days in the late 80s, early 90s, and we didn't have a lot of devices, my parents couldn't afford, but those things that you then finally got your hands on were obviously premium titles that were paid, and sometimes you would have spent 60, 70 dollars on a game that you didn't like.

474
00:45:35,360 --> 00:45:39,200
And now you can get a ton of hours of entertainment out of 60 or 70 dollars.

475
00:45:39,840 --> 00:45:50,320
So I think even if you then spent 8 or 10 dollars on a pass, that's a lot of entertainment if that unlocked a lot of rewards in a game that you like.

476
00:45:51,360 --> 00:45:57,680
So if the trade is fair fair, if you feel that it's transparent what you get for that, then I actually really like the season pass idea.

477
00:45:58,160 --> 00:46:10,480
And then, of course, if your app is a utility or something that you either need, but for example, when I'm, I signed up for a marathon and I want to say it as many places as possible, I will do the marathon.

478
00:46:11,200 --> 00:46:15,680
But I would love an app that said, I will get you to the marathon, but I will not charge you for two years after.

479
00:46:16,320 --> 00:46:20,720
If at the end of that said, let me become an annual subscriber, then I like that transaction.

480
00:46:21,600 --> 00:46:24,720
But I think back to the scanning app, then sometimes you just need one.

481
00:46:25,360 --> 00:46:34,800
scan, but you have to sign up for 800 krona or 100 dollars to get that one thing or a monthly thing that reactivates and you forget about it.

482
00:46:35,120 --> 00:46:39,440
And I think the season pass, figuring out what is the right duration of time.

483
00:46:40,800 --> 00:46:55,280
Is this a one day, do everything you want, is this a one week, is this in Subway Surface three weeks for this season or in most other apps a monthly thing that happens and then you choose whether this also is a month where you feel that this is worth paying for.

484
00:46:55,600 --> 00:46:58,000
Well, Matthias, it's been so fun chatting with you.

485
00:46:58,080 --> 00:46:59,840
I feel like we could chat another two hours.

486
00:47:00,240 --> 00:47:03,520
Honestly, it's been an absolute pleasure having this conversation.

487
00:47:03,760 --> 00:47:09,760
And again, I think so many things that subscription apps and the kind of productivity side can learn from gaming.

488
00:47:10,320 --> 00:47:12,160
And I think you've demonstrated that today.

489
00:47:12,640 --> 00:47:13,680
So many great lessons.

490
00:47:14,400 --> 00:47:16,480
But anything else you wanted to share as we wrap up?

491
00:47:16,800 --> 00:47:18,240
I think it's been a really good conversation.

492
00:47:18,480 --> 00:47:26,240
And I appreciate the question because it also makes me think how games are far in some areas, but also other areas where we can definitely learn from apps.

493
00:47:26,560 --> 00:47:28,960
And I'm happy to come back another time to discuss another hour.

494
00:47:29,520 --> 00:47:50,800
I think my main thing is just that the monetization and the gamification, that term is sometimes used a bit too widely, is about figuring out how you make the player journey or the user journey as frictionless as possible with a few friction points that also makes it clear that here you have the value points if you want to make those decisions.

495
00:47:51,520 --> 00:47:54,080
And I think in general, it's hard to make games.

496
00:47:54,240 --> 00:47:55,760
So we're not saying it's easy either.

497
00:47:56,080 --> 00:48:06,320
I know it's also hard to make apps, but I encourage everyone who has an idea to try it because I really like the creativity that comes when people become entrepreneurial in areas that can help others.

498
00:48:06,880 --> 00:48:07,200
Awesome.

499
00:48:07,360 --> 00:48:08,960
Well, thank you so much for joining us today.

500
00:48:09,120 --> 00:48:10,320
This was a really fun conversation.

501
00:48:11,120 --> 00:48:11,440
Thank you.

502
00:48:11,680 --> 00:48:12,000
Thank you.

503
00:48:14,720 --> 00:48:16,080
Thank you so much for listening.

504
00:48:16,960 --> 00:48:20,400
If you have a minute, please leave a review in your favorite podcast player.

